# QSP first-run settings. Copy to .env and edit.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Both are read only when there is no configuration file yet. After the first
# start, /var/lib/qsp/qsp.json is yours and nothing here overwrites it.

# The shared secret your hotspots use to log in.
#
# You choose it, and you put the same one into Pi-Star or WPSD under the
# master's password. There is no default: a master whose password everybody
# knows has no password.
QSP_PEER_PASSWORD=change-this-to-something-long

# The IDs allowed to register, comma-separated.
#
# Use the ID shown on your hotspot's dashboard, which is usually your own
# seven-digit ID. A Motorola repeater registers with a six-digit ID.
#
# QSP may print an advisory about seven-digit IDs at startup. It is a prompt to
# check, not an error: hotspots registering with a plain seven-digit ID are
# ordinary, and the advisory exists because some append a two-digit suffix.
#
# QSP will not start an open master for you: a listener reachable from the
# internet that accepts anybody is a problem for the people it relays to as
# much as for you.
#
# Ranges work too: 3132910,3155000-3155999
QSP_ALLOWED_PEERS=3132910
